    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg_01M508XKD9YJG2677H8WJK902S",
      "thread_id": "thr_01M508X9PC9K9HKHSVB2J9W1A1",
      "ordinal": 3,
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The paper's reason is about sequencing, not about raw arithmetic [b6].\n\nRecurrent models \"factor computation along the symbol positions of the input and output sequences,\" aligning sequence positions to steps in computation time: each hidden state h_t is a function of the previous hidden state h_t−1 and the input at position t [b6]. Because step t cannot be computed before step t−1 exists, this \"inherently sequential nature precludes parallelization within training examples\" [b6].\n\nDropping recurrence removes that ordering constraint, so positions no longer have to wait on one another. The abstract reports the payoff as models that are \"more parallelizable and requiring significantly less time to train\" [b1], and the Introduction attributes this directly to the architecture: the Transformer \"allows for significantly more parallelization\" [b8].\n\nTwo further points from the paper. First, this matters most at long sequence lengths, \"as memory constraints limit batching across examples\" — so the sequential bottleneck also blocks the workaround of running many examples at once [b6]. Second, the limitation was recognized as fundamental: tricks like factorization and conditional computation had already improved efficiency, but \"the fundamental constraint of sequential computation, however, remains\" [b6].\n\nNote the paper frames parallelism as the mechanism and cost as the consequence; it does not here quantify the speedup beyond the training-time figures already quoted [b1, b8].",
